<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edict of Expulsion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1290">1290</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edict of Expulsion was a royal decree issued by Edward I on 18 July 1290 that expelled all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the first time a European state is known to have permanently banned their presence. It followed punitive taxation, the Statute of the Jewry of 1275, which outlawed lending at interest, and the death sentences on over 300 Jews in the coin-clipping crisis of the late 1270s. The Jews, perhaps as few as 2,000, could take cash and personal possessions, but their debts, homes, synagogues and cemeteries were forfeited to the king, and some died through piracy or drowned in the Channel. It stood until 1656, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> informally permitted their resettlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edict-of-expulsion.jpg" alt="Edict of Expulsion" width="330" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BritLibCottonNeroDiiFol183vPersecutedJews.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
